            <div xml:id="d331">

                <pb facs="0684" n="652" xml:id="pg652"/>

                <head>331. Memorandum of Conversation<anchor n="1" xml:id="d331-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1968-02-15">February 15,
                            1968</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Laos</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS</label>
                    <item>The Secretary</item>
                    <item>Deputy Under Secretary Charles E.
                            Bohlen
                    </item>
                    <item>Soviet Ambassador Anatoliy Dobrynin
                    </item>
                </list>
                <p>During an hour and a half’s luncheon conversation the following point was touched
                    on:</p>
                <p>The Secretary said that our recent information showed that there were North
                    Vietnamese battalions around the town of Saravane in southern Laos and that if,
                    as was possible, the North Vietnamese launched an attack on the city, the U.S.
                    would become militarily involved. Dobrynin made a note of the name Saravane, obviously for the
                    purpose of informing Moscow. The Secretary said that he thought the U.K. and the
                    Soviet Union as co-chairmen of the 1962 Laos arrangement had certain duties,
                    pointing out to Dobrynin that this
                    agreement had been made quite independently of events in Viet-Nam. Dobrynin agreed.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
